@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')
  Syntax overview
@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>Propel's syntax follows Blade. Each part below has a page with examples.</p>

  <h2>Output</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>@{{ $name }}</code> prints a value, escaped for HTML. See <a href="/features/escaping">escaping</a>.</li>
    <li>The raw-output tag, <code>@{!! $html !!}</code>, prints a value without escaping. Use it only for trusted content.</li>
    <li>A comment goes between the echo braces, wrapped in two dashes on each side. It is not sent to the browser.</li>
    <li><code>@{{</code> prints a literal <code>@{{</code>, and <code>@{!!</code> prints a literal <code>@{!!</code>, for code samples and JavaScript.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Conditions</h2>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@if ($user)
  Hello, @{{ $user.name }}
@@elseif ($guest)
  Welcome
@@else
  Please sign in
@@endif</code></pre>
  <p>A value is false when it is <code>null</code> or <code>false</code>, zero, an empty string, or an empty list or object. See <a href="/features/control">control flow</a>.</p>

  <h2>Loops</h2>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@foreach (post in posts)
  @{{ $post.title }}
@@endforeach

@@forelse (post in posts)
  @{{ $post.title }}
@@empty
  No posts yet.
@@endforelse</code></pre>
  <p>A <code>null</code> list counts as empty, so <code>@@empty</code> shows.</p>

  <h2>Layouts and sections</h2>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@extends('layouts.app')

@@section('content')
  The page content.
@@endsection</code></pre>
  <p>The layout places each section where it calls <code>@@yield('content')</code>. A section must be at the top level of the view, not inside an <code>@@if</code> or a loop.</p>
  <p>Defaults, <code>@@parent</code>, <code>@@show</code>, partials with <code>@@include</code>, and stacks are on the <a href="/docs/layouts">layouts and sections</a> page.</p>

  <h2>Assets</h2>
  <p><code>asset('resources/images/logo.svg')</code> returns the URL of a file, and <code>@@vite([...])</code> loads entries. See <a href="/docs/assets">images, fonts and assets</a>.</p>

  <h2>Debugging</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>@@dump($value)</code> prints a value and keeps rendering. See <a href="/features/dump">@@dump</a>.</li>
    <li><code>@@dd($value)</code> is "dump and die", as in Laravel: it prints a value and stops rendering the section it is in. The layout still finishes, so the page loads. See <a href="/features/dd">@@dd</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Debug helpers render only in development builds.</p>

  <h2>Writing a directive as text</h2>
  <p>A directive word in plain text needs two @ signs in front of it in the template, so Propel prints one. Email addresses and CSS rules such as <code>@media</code> need no escape, because Propel only reads the directive words it knows.</p>
@endsection
